.. Online documentation can be found at: https://winterlandcosmology.bitbucket.io/pychfpga

Introduction
============

The ``pychfpga`` package is a Python framework for operating arrays of FPGA boards used in implementing radiotelescope arrays of various sizes, from a single-board desktop correlator up to arrays of hundreds of crate-mounted boards forming a multi-thousand element interferometer.

``pychfpga`` can operate platforms that run the chFPGA firmware (https://winterlandcosmology.bitbucket.io/chfpga/). Currently supported platforms are:

- Xilinx KC705: (FPGA: Kintex 7). Off-the-shelf evaluation board. Needs one or two McGill MGADC08 8-channel ADC boards.
- Xilinx ZCU111 (FPGA: Ultrascale+ RFSoC). Off-the-shelf evaluation board. Needs ADC breakout board.
- McGill IceBoard: (FPGA Xilinx Kintex 7). Custom board designed by McGill University for large synchronized arrays. Needs one or two McGill MGADC08 8-channel ADC boards.
- t0 CRS (Ultrascale+ RFSoC). Custom board designed by ``t0 technology`` (https://www.t0.technology) for large synchronized arrays.

``pychfpga`` uploads the chFPGA firmware to the FPGA and initializes all of its subsystems, including:

- ADC data acquisition (including calibration)
- F-Engine (channelizer) including a function generator, PFB/FFT, Scaler and raw data capture
- Corner-turn engine for transposing data between 1 board, 1 crate (16 boards) or 2 crates (32 boards)
- Optional on-board X-Engine (N^2 correlator): (4 + 4j) bit data width
- Optional 40- or 100 Gbps Ethernet real-time data offload to an external X-Engine farm.

``pychfpga`` is currently used to operate telescopes such as CHIME, CHORD, HIRAX, various outriggers of the previous telescopes, and a number of small portable interferometer-based experiments.


Features
========

- Multi-platform: Supports multiple hardware platforms

- Hardware map manager: Keeps track of all the boards, crates and mezzanine boards in an array and
  how they connect together.

- Auto-discovery: FPGA boards can be added to the hardware map explicitly by specifying their IP
  address, or can be automatically found on the local network using mDNS based on the board or crate
  serial numbers. The boards can query the hardware to find the model and serial number of the
  motherboard, crate and mezzanines.

- Concurrent operation: The array manager can perform operations on multiple boards at the same time
  using asynchronous coroutines to maximize performance.

- Array-wide synchronization: Provides methods to perform array-wide data acquisition synchronization
  based on GPS-provided timestamps

- Multiple operational modes: The FPGA can be configured in various ways (correlator, baseband streaming etc.).
  The proper FPGA firmware for the desired operational mode and platform is automatically selected,
  uploaded to the FPGA, and initialized.

``pychfpga`` can be used both in interactive or automated modes:

- Interactive mode: the user instantiates :class:`pychfpga.fpga_array.FPGAArray`
  with a hardware map, operational mode, etc., then talks to the array or
  individual boards from ``ipython`` or a Jupyter notebook. See
  :doc:`howto/fpga_array`.

- Automated mode: :mod:`pychfpga.fpga_master` identifies and initializes the
  array from a YAML file and serves REST commands plus Prometheus metrics.
  See :doc:`howto/server_operation`.


Documentation
=============

This README is the package overview. Operational how-tos live in the Sphinx
manual (build with ``make docs`` from the repository root):

- :doc:`howto/ssh_keys` — Bitbucket SSH keys
- :doc:`howto/installation` — Python environment and ``pip install``
- :doc:`howto/hardware_setup` — IceBoard wiring, clock, and Ethernet
- :doc:`howto/fpga_array` — interactive ``fpga_array``
- :doc:`howto/server_operation` — ``fpga_master`` / ``raw_acq`` / Docker
- :doc:`howto/testing` — ice-qc hardware QC and firmware tests


Requirements
============

``pychfpga`` and many of its dependencies are hosted in a private git
repository on Bitbucket. You need an account, an SSH key attached to that
account, and permission to the repositories. See :doc:`howto/ssh_keys`.

System packages: Git (with LFS), Python 3.8+ (QC tests have issues with 3.9;
3.10 has known issues), ``pip`` >= 18.1, and the matching ``pythonX.X-dev``
package (needed to compile ``netifaces``).

Install Prometheus and Grafana if you want the graphs published by
``fpga_master``.

If you will receive raw ADC, FFT, or correlator packets, enable jumbo frames
and enlarge UDP buffers (session example; make this permanent if needed)::

    sudo ifconfig eth0 mtu 9000
    sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_max=26214400
    sudo sysctl -w net.core.rmem_default=26214400

FPGA hardware, clock, and Ethernet requirements are in
:doc:`howto/hardware_setup`.


Installation
============

See :doc:`howto/installation` for platform-specific notes. The short path::

    git clone git@bitbucket.org:winterlandcosmology/pychfpga.git
    python3 -m venv my_env
    source my_env/bin/activate
    pip install -e ./pychfpga
    pip install ipython

Quality-control extras::

    pip install -e ./pychfpga[qc]
